-- spat.rofl — SPAT: the rules. Your week lives in week.rofl; nothing here
-- needs editing to describe a different family.
--
-- BRUTE FORCE COMPUTES, ROFL EXPLAINS. Nothing here is a solver. These rules
-- materialise the whole picture — where everyone is, minute by minute, who is
-- covering which child, how much slack every handover has, which
-- responsibilities have a backup and which do not — and name, for every hole,
-- THE CONSTRAINT AND ITS OWNER. Choosing among what survives is a search over
-- a handful of items and lives in spat.ts, where CP-SAT could replace it
-- without touching a line of this file.
--
-- THE CENTRAL PREDICATE IS COVERAGE. `uncovered(Child, Day, Slot)` is the red
-- block in a hand-built schedule: the child is awake, is not at school or
-- садик, and there is nobody to be with them. Everything else in this file
-- either feeds it or reads it. Feasibility is the wider question and coverage
-- is one component of it — `broken/1` collects all of them.
--
-- TWO CLOCKS, ON PURPOSE:
--   * COVERAGE runs on a 20-minute grid (`slot/2`). Coverage is a
--     point-in-time property of a whole day, and materialising it is what
--     makes `uncovered`, `sole`, free time and effective hours all fall out of
--     one model instead of four.
--   * SLACK is exact integer arithmetic on the endpoints themselves. "Физио
--     ends 13:20, pickup starts 13:25" is a five-minute fact and rounding it
--     to a grid would destroy the answer that matters most.
--
-- ENGINE DISCIPLINES, all three load-bearing and all three tested:
--   * NO @next TICKS. The day is an ordinary fact argument; the WEEK is
--     selected by one `current/1` fact, re-seeded and evaluated at tick 0.
--     Carrying facts across ticks would make every one its own support one
--     tick back and the counting semiring would say "infinitely many" for
--     every responsibility in the house.
--   * EVERY RULE RANGE-RESTRICTED in written premise order, so everything is
--     materialised and the semirings fold over the same world the Boolean
--     answers came from.
--   * PREMISE ORDER IS PERFORMANCE. The engine has no argument index: every
--     premise lookup scans its whole relation, so a rule costs
--     (rows the prefix yields) x (size of the next relation). Where a rule
--     below looks written backwards, that is why, and the comment says so.

-- =========================================================================
-- 0. THE CALENDAR AND THE GRID
-- =========================================================================
-- The whole weekday difference comes from `applies_on/2`: a day field is one
-- day, the atom `weekdays`, `alldays`, or a group named with `in_group/2`.
-- There are no seven schedules anywhere.

weekday(D)              :- day(D, N), N <= 5.
applies_on(D, D)        :- day(D, _).
applies_on(weekdays, D) :- weekday(D).
applies_on(alldays, D)  :- day(D, _).
applies_on(G, D)        :- in_group(G, D), day(D, _).

-- The coverage grid. `hh`/`mm` are crossed once rather than counted up
-- recursively: 90 slots a day generated in one firing instead of 90 rounds.
hh(7).  hh(8).  hh(9).  hh(10). hh(11). hh(12). hh(13). hh(14).
hh(15). hh(16). hh(17). hh(18). hh(19). hh(20). hh(21).
mm(0). mm(20). mm(40).
grid(20).
day_end(1320).
slot(D, T) :- day(D, _), hh(H), mm(M), T is H * 60 + M.

-- =========================================================================
-- 1. CONSTRAINTS BELONG TO PEOPLE
-- =========================================================================
-- `constraint(Id, Owner, Scope)` is required for every constraint id used
-- anywhere. Scope `external` marks what the household does not own — the
-- садик's closing time, the school bus, the neighbours, the employer. Those
-- change without warning and are the source of nearly every perturbation.
--
-- The user's own question — "садик до 13:30: это решение или ограничение?" —
-- is answered by the owner field and by nothing else.

owner(C, O)        :- constraint(C, O, _).
external(C)        :- constraint(C, _, external).
household(C)       :- constraint(C, _, household).
has_give(C)        :- give(C, _).
give_cost(C, N)    :- give(C, N).
give_cost(C, 50)   :- constraint(C, _, household), not has_give(C).
give_cost(C, 200)  :- constraint(C, _, external), not has_give(C).

-- `waived(C)` is how `relax` asks "and if this one gave?". Every rule that
-- applies a constraint reads `not waived(C)`, so waiving is a data change.

-- =========================================================================
-- 2. THE WEEK: ONE TYPICAL PATTERN, PLUS WHAT THIS WEEK DID DIFFERENTLY
-- =========================================================================
-- Four layers, in order of authority:
--   usual/7      the typical week — the world's lines, and a household's
--                recurring edits (access.rofl §5, `e_usual`)
--   usual_on/5   a day that is always different (the Friday bus)
--   moved/7      this week only, possibly to a different day
--   skipped/4    this week only, not on at all
--   added/8      this week only, a one-off
--
-- `current(Week)` selects which dated week is in force. Swapping it and
-- re-evaluating is the as-of pattern: the same ledger, a different lens, one
-- evaluation at tick 0.

usual_on_day(Ev, D) :- usual_on(_, Ev, D, _, _).
week_changed(Ev, D) :- moved(_, Ev, W, D, _, _, _), current(W).
week_changed(Ev, D) :- skipped(_, Ev, W, D), current(W).

-- `person(W, _)` is what makes a misspelt name lose its week rather than
-- quietly acquiring one nobody can see. `spat` lints for exactly that.
span(C, Ev, W, P, D, F, T) :- usual(C, Ev, W, P, Spec, F, T), person(W, _),
                              applies_on(Spec, D),
                              not usual_on_day(Ev, D), not week_changed(Ev, D),
                              not waived(C).
span(C, Ev, W, P, D, F, T) :- usual(C, Ev, W, P, Spec, _, _), person(W, _),
                              applies_on(Spec, D),
                              usual_on(C2, Ev, D, F, T), not week_changed(Ev, D),
                              not waived(C), not waived(C2).
span(C, Ev, W, P, D2, F, T) :- moved(C2, Ev, Wk, D, D2, F, T), current(Wk),
                               usual(C, Ev, W, P, Spec, _, _), person(W, _),
                               applies_on(Spec, D), not waived(C), not waived(C2).
span(C, Ev, W, P, D, F, T) :- added(C, Ev, W, P, Wk, D, F, T), person(W, _),
                              current(Wk), not waived(C).

-- =========================================================================
-- 3. WHERE EVERYONE IS
-- =========================================================================
-- Travel is real and it is derived, never typed. A block away from base costs
-- the journey out before it and the journey back after it, and those minutes
-- are exactly what turns "плавание until 18:00" into "not home until 18:20".

-- TRAVEL IS NOT A NUMBER, and pretending it is puts a lie under every slack
-- in the week. The same drive varies with traffic, with the route taken, and
-- with how hard the driver is pressing; a figure measured on a quiet morning
-- by the long way round is ONE SAMPLE OF ONE CONDITION.
--
-- So a condition is named, and `assume/1` picks which one the week is being
-- read under - exactly the shape `current/1` already has for weeks. With no
-- `travel_as` fact for a pair, `travel/3` still answers, so a household that
-- never measured a second condition pays nothing and nothing changes.
--
-- The condition name carries route AND style together on purpose: what the
-- schedule needs is the minutes, and `long_easy` versus `yards_brisk` is the
-- distinction a person can actually report.

-- И ЕЩЁ ОДИН УРОВЕНЬ ВЫШЕ ДОПУЩЕНИЯ СТОИТ ЗАМЕР. `ride(Когда, Откуда, Куда,
-- Минут)` — одна состоявшаяся поездка, одна строка. Спрошено прямо: «стоит
-- трекать успеваю или не успеваю или за сколько доехали». Тогда время дороги
-- перестаёт быть константой, которую кто-то однажды вписал, и становится
-- ДИАПАЗОНОМ по наблюдениям: он сам сужается, сам расширяется, и каждое его
-- число имеет дату.
--
-- Порядок старшинства ровно один и он проверяем: замер > условие > оценка.
-- Пара, по которой ездили, больше не слушает ни travel_as, ни travel.
--
-- Ядро не умеет агрегировать, но КРАЙНИЕ точки берутся отрицанием, а не
-- сложением: самый медленный замер — это тот, медленнее которого нет. Ровно
-- та же фигура, что `tightest` в тропическом полукольце, только без фолда.
slower(A, B, M)  :- ride(_, A, B, M), ride(_, A, B, M2), M2 > M.
faster(A, B, M)  :- ride(_, A, B, M), ride(_, A, B, M2), M2 < M.
slowest(A, B, M) :- ride(_, A, B, M), not slower(A, B, M).
fastest(A, B, M) :- ride(_, A, B, M), not faster(A, B, M).

rode(A, B) :- ride(_, A, B, _).
rode(B, A) :- ride(_, A, B, _), A != B.

-- ПО ХУДШЕМУ ИЗ НАБЛЮДЁННЫХ, потому что вопрос стоит «влезет ли», а не
-- «сколько обычно». Быстрый заезд не двигает расписание — он расширяет
-- диапазон вниз, и это видно отдельной строкой в отчёте.
tt(A, B, M) :- slowest(A, B, M).
tt(B, A, M) :- slowest(A, B, M), A != B.

ride_range(A, B, Lo, Hi) :- fastest(A, B, Lo), slowest(A, B, Hi), Lo != Hi.

tt(A, B, M) :- assume(C), travel_as(C, A, B, M), not rode(A, B).
tt(B, A, M) :- assume(C), travel_as(C, A, B, M), A != B, not rode(B, A).
tt(A, B, M) :- travel(A, B, M), not measured(A, B), not rode(A, B).
tt(B, A, M) :- travel(A, B, M), A != B, not measured(A, B), not rode(B, A).
tt(A, A, 0) :- place(A).

-- ПЕШКОМ НЕ БЫВАЕТ БЫСТРЕЕ, ЧЕМ НА МАШИНЕ, и равно тоже не бывает. Если
-- такая пара нашлась — одно из двух чисел выдумано, и модель обязана назвать
-- пару, а не молча считать по ней запас. Это НЕ `broken`: расписание тут ни
-- при чём, спорят данные.
faster_on_foot(A, B, F, T) :- foot(A, B, F), tt(A, B, T), F <= T.

-- РОВНО ОДНО ДОПУЩЕНИЕ, и это не пожелание в комментарии. Два `assume` дают
-- одной паре два времени сразу, и дальше КАЖДЫЙ запас в неделе считается по
-- тому из них, которое подвернулось первым — ответ остаётся булевым и
-- правдоподобным, а числа под ним описывают разные миры. Ядро не умеет
-- сказать «ровно один», но пару с двумя числами оно называет, и этого хватает.
two_ways(A, B, M1, M2) :- tt(A, B, M1), tt(A, B, M2), M1 < M2.

measured(A, B) :- assume(C), travel_as(C, A, B, _).
measured(B, A) :- assume(C), travel_as(C, A, B, _).

-- HOW MUCH DRIVING A HANDOVER CAN ABSORB. `slack` says how many minutes are
-- left over; this says the whole envelope, so a measurement can be placed
-- inside it: "today took 25 of the 25 there are" is a different sentence from
-- "today took 25 minutes".
travel_budget(P, D, E1, E2, Max) :-
    adjacent(P, D, E1, E2), pair_next(P, D, E1, E2, End1, _),
    latest_start(E2, D, L), Max is L - End1.

away(P, D, C) :- absent(C, P, Spec), applies_on(Spec, D), not waived(C).
away(P, D, C) :- absent_on(C, P, Wk, D), current(Wk), day(D, _), not waived(C).
off(P, D)     :- away(P, D, _).

-- OUT: physically not at base, journey included. This is what blocks
-- coverage, and it is deliberately NOT the same as being busy: a parent
-- working from home is busy and still covering.
--
-- `out_why` carries the CONSTRAINT that put them there, which is the whole
-- reason this example exists: a red block has to name the assertion that
-- caused it and whose it is, and that attribution belongs in the rules, not
-- in whatever renders them.
out_why(P, D, S, C) :- span(C, _, P, Pl, D, F, T), base(B), Pl != B,
                       slot(D, S), S >= F, S < T.
out_why(P, D, S, C) :- span(C, _, P, Pl, D, F, T), base(B), Pl != B, tt(B, Pl, M),
                       slot(D, S), Pre is F - M, S >= Pre, S < F.
out_why(P, D, S, C) :- span(C, _, P, Pl, D, F, T), base(B), Pl != B, tt(Pl, B, M),
                       slot(D, S), Post is T + M, S >= T, S < Post.
out(P, D, S)        :- out_why(P, D, S, _).

-- BUSY: committed to anything at all, wherever it happens. This is what free
-- time is measured against — and only free time and placement read it, so it
-- is gated: materialising it always costs about a thousand facts, and in this
-- engine the store's sorted insert makes fact count, not join count, the
-- thing that costs.
deep(slots) :- asking(free).
deep(slots) :- asking(hours).
deep(slots) :- want(_, _, _, _).
busy(P, D, S) :- deep(slots), span(_, _, P, _, D, F, T), slot(D, S), S >= F, S < T.
busy(P, D, S) :- deep(slots), out(P, D, S).

-- =========================================================================
-- 4. COVERAGE — the central predicate
-- =========================================================================

coverer(P) :- person(P, adult).
coverer(P) :- person(P, helper).
coverer(P) :- person(P, neighbour).

-- A helper or a neighbour exists only inside a declared window; everyone else
-- is around all day unless away.
--
-- A WINDOW IS PER DAY. The world's line stands on its days; a dated exception
-- a book wrote — «няня в среду с 17:30» (access.rofl §5, `present_on/5`) — is
-- that person's window for THAT day instead of the world's, as `moved` stands
-- instead of `usual`, never beside it: the world's 19:00–22:00 does not go on
-- covering an evening the person said they would come at 17:30 and leave at 21:00.
bounded(P)    :- present_window(_, P, _, _, _).
window(C, P, D, F, T) :- present_window(C, P, Spec, F, T), applies_on(Spec, D),
                         not window_changed(P, D).
window(C, P, D, F, T) :- present_on(C, P, D, F, T).
window_changed(P, D)  :- present_on(_, P, D, _, _).
here(P, D, S) :- needy(D, S), coverer(P), window(C, P, D, F, T),
                 S >= F, S < T, not waived(C), not off(P, D).
here(P, D, S) :- needy(D, S), coverer(P), not bounded(P), not off(P, D).

-- Restricted to the slots where a child actually needs somebody: an adult
-- being free at 09:00 on a Wednesday while both children are at school is a
-- true fact about nothing.
on_duty(P, D, S) :- needy(D, S), here(P, D, S), not out(P, D, S).

-- ONE DERIVATION PER PERSON ON DUTY. The counting semiring folded over this
-- is the backup number, and it is the same metric NOPE uses for privilege
-- sprawl with the sign inverted: there many independent paths is the alarm,
-- here many is the reassurance and ONE is the alarm.
any_duty(D, S)   :- on_duty(_, D, S).

-- A child needs looking after when awake, not somewhere supervised, and not
-- already with an adult whose block says so (`with/2`).
awake_at(Ch, D, S) :- awake(C, Ch, Spec, F, T), applies_on(Spec, D),
                      slot(D, S), S >= F, S < T, not waived(C).
at_sup(Ch, D, S)   :- span(_, _, Ch, Pl, D, F, T), supervised(Pl),
                      slot(D, S), S >= F, S < T.
-- A child in somebody's `with` block is with them for the JOURNEY too, not
-- only for the block. Without the two travel rules the model reports a
-- twenty-minute hole at the end of every outing, which is an artefact of the
-- grid and not a fact about the family.
minding(P, Ch, D, S) :- span(_, Ev, P, _, D, F, T), with(Ev, Ch),
                        slot(D, S), S >= F, S < T.
minding(P, Ch, D, S) :- span(_, Ev, P, Pl, D, F, _), with(Ev, Ch), base(B), Pl != B,
                        tt(B, Pl, M), slot(D, S), Pre is F - M, S >= Pre, S < F.
minding(P, Ch, D, S) :- span(_, Ev, P, Pl, D, _, T), with(Ev, Ch), base(B), Pl != B,
                        tt(Pl, B, M), slot(D, S), Post is T + M, S >= T, S < Post.
minded(Ch, D, S)   :- minding(_, Ch, D, S).

needs_cover(Ch, D, S) :- awake_at(Ch, D, S), not at_sup(Ch, D, S), not minded(Ch, D, S).

-- AND NOT ALONE WHEN ASLEEP EITHER. `awake` says when a child needs somebody
-- to be WITH them; it does not say when the house may be empty, and those are
-- different sentences. Measured: with both adults out from 21:00 and both
-- children at home, this model reported ZERO holes, because nico's awake
-- window closes at 20:00 - while the same departure at 15:00 reported two.
-- The probe could tell the difference, so the zero was real.
--
-- `never_alone(Child)` is opt-in per child, because a fifteen-year-old is a
-- different question from a five-year-old. The grid runs 07:00-21:40, so this
-- adds exactly the evening the awake window drops and no night at all.
needs_cover(Ch, D, S) :- never_alone(Ch), person(Ch, child), slot(D, S),
                         not at_sup(Ch, D, S), not minded(Ch, D, S).

-- `needy` must have exactly ONE derivation per slot. Written the obvious way
-- (`needy(D,S) :- needs_cover(_,D,S)`) it has one per CHILD, and that
-- multiplicity rides all the way through `here` and `on_duty` into the
-- counting semiring, which then reports two coverers as four. Routing it
-- through a negation collapses it: finite failure carries no annotation.
no_need(D, S)         :- slot(D, S), not needs_cover(_, D, S).
needy(D, S)           :- slot(D, S), not no_need(D, S).
uncovered(Ch, D, S)   :- needs_cover(Ch, D, S), not any_duty(D, S).

-- SOLE COVER: on duty, with nobody else who could take over. This is the
-- predicate free time and effective working hours are both subtracted by, and
-- it is why "работа 08:30-13:00" is really three or four hours.
--
-- `backed` is written pivot-first on purpose: `pairc` has six rows, so the
-- rule costs six scans of `duty` instead of one scan per duty row. Written
-- the natural way round (duty, duty, P != Q) the same answer costs a
-- thousand times more work — measured, 15.6 s against 0.02 s.
-- Gated with `busy` for the same reason: only free time, working hours and
-- placement read sole cover, and each of those announces itself.
pairc(P, Q)     :- coverer(P), coverer(Q), P != Q.
backed(P, D, S) :- deep(slots), pairc(P, Q), on_duty(Q, D, S).
sole(P, D, S)   :- deep(slots), on_duty(P, D, S), not backed(P, D, S).

-- =========================================================================
-- 5. CHAINS AND SLACK — "works, with not one minute to spare"
-- =========================================================================
-- Two consecutive commitments of the same person on the same day, and the
-- minutes left over once the journey between them is paid for. Slack may be
-- zero, and it may be negative: a Boolean feasibility answer says YES to both
-- and that is exactly what it conceals.

pair_next(P, D, E1, E2, End1, Start2) :-
    span(_, E1, P, _, D, _, End1), span(_, E2, P, _, D, Start2, _),
    E1 != E2, End1 <= Start2.
crowded(P, D, E1, E2) :-
    pair_next(P, D, E1, E2, End1, Start2), span(_, E3, P, _, D, S3, _),
    E3 != E1, E3 != E2, End1 < S3, S3 < Start2.
adjacent(P, D, E1, E2) :- pair_next(P, D, E1, E2, _, _), not crowded(P, D, E1, E2).

-- A TIME IS NOT ALWAYS A TIME. Some blocks must begin exactly when they say;
-- others have a WINDOW - "Нико можно сдать с 7:45 до 8:15" - and some have
-- only a far edge - "Кит должен быть у остановки не позднее 7:45". Inside a
-- window there is room to manoeuvre, and a model that reads the observed
-- arrival as the requirement CANNOT SEE THAT ROOM. It reported zero slack on a
-- morning that had twenty minutes in hand, because the drop-off was recorded
-- at the minute it happened rather than the minute it was due.
--
-- `must_start(C, Ev, Earliest, Latest)` states the window. A deadline is a
-- window whose early edge is 0. With no such fact the latest start IS the
-- written start, so a week that declares no window is unchanged to the byte.

has_window(C, Ev)      :- must_start(C, Ev, _, _).
latest_start(Ev, D, L) :- must_start(C, Ev, _, L), span(C, Ev, _, _, D, _, _).
latest_start(Ev, D, F) :- span(C, Ev, _, _, D, F, _), not has_window(C, Ev).

too_early(C, Ev, D, F, By) :- must_start(C, Ev, E, _), span(C, Ev, _, _, D, F, _),
                              F < E, By is E - F.
too_late(C, Ev, D, F, By)  :- must_start(C, Ev, _, L), span(C, Ev, _, _, D, F, _),
                              F > L, By is F - L.

-- SLACK IS MEASURED TO THE LATEST ALLOWED START, not to the written one. That
-- is the whole correction: how late you may leave and still be in time.
slack(P, D, E1, E2, M) :-
    adjacent(P, D, E1, E2), pair_next(P, D, E1, E2, End1, _),
    span(_, E1, P, Pl1, D, _, End1), span(_, E2, P, Pl2, D, _, _),
    latest_start(E2, D, L), tt(Pl1, Pl2, TT), M is L - End1 - TT.

-- One derivation per chain, so the tropical semiring folded over this gives
-- the MINIMUM slack of the day — an aggregation the kernel deliberately does
-- not have (START.md section 8) and the semiring supplies exactly.
day_tight(P, D) :- slack(P, D, _, _, _).

zero_slack(P, D, E1, E2)     :- slack(P, D, E1, E2, 0).
negative_slack(P, D, E1, E2) :- slack(P, D, E1, E2, M), M < 0.
tight(P, D, E1, E2)          :- slack(P, D, E1, E2, M), M <= 0.

-- A gap with no slack is not free time: any overrun eats it.
in_tight_gap(P, D, S) :- tight(P, D, E1, E2), pair_next(P, D, E1, E2, End1, Start2),
                         slot(D, S), S >= End1, S < Start2.

-- =========================================================================
-- 6. RUNS: getting a child from one place to another
-- =========================================================================
-- Derived from the child's own day, not typed: wherever a child's
-- commitments change place — and at the two ends of the day, to and from
-- base — somebody has to make that happen.

has_prev(Ch, D, E2) :- adjacent(Ch, D, _, E2).
has_next(Ch, D, E1) :- adjacent(Ch, D, E1, _).

-- A run whose arrival is already inside an adult's `with` block is not a
-- run: somebody wrote the school run down, and that IS the answer.
carried_at(Ch, D, Pl, At) :- span(_, _, Ch, Pl, D, At, _), person(Ch, child),
                             span(_, Ev, P, Pl, D, F, X), with(Ev, Ch),
                             person(P, adult), F <= At, At <= X.
carried_lv(Ch, D, Pl, Lv) :- span(_, _, Ch, Pl, D, _, Lv), person(Ch, child),
                             span(_, Ev, P, Pl, D, F, X), with(Ev, Ch),
                             person(P, adult), F <= Lv, Lv <= X.

run(go(Ev, D), Ch, B, Pl, D, arrive_by, Arr) :-
    span(_, Ev, Ch, Pl, D, Arr, _), person(Ch, child), base(B), Pl != B,
    not has_prev(Ch, D, Ev), not carried_at(Ch, D, Pl, Arr).
run(back(Ev, D), Ch, Pl, B, D, depart_at, Lv) :-
    span(_, Ev, Ch, Pl, D, _, Lv), person(Ch, child), base(B), Pl != B,
    not has_next(Ch, D, Ev), not carried_lv(Ch, D, Pl, Lv).
-- a hop is answered at either end: somebody meets the child where the next
-- block starts, or somebody takes them from where the last one ended
run(hop(E1, E2, D), Ch, P1, P2, D, arrive_by, Arr) :-
    adjacent(Ch, D, E1, E2), person(Ch, child),
    span(_, E1, Ch, P1, D, _, Lv), span(_, E2, Ch, P2, D, Arr, _), P1 != P2,
    not carried_at(Ch, D, P2, Arr), not carried_lv(Ch, D, P1, Lv).

-- The ways a run could happen. `attempt` is shape only; what kills one is
-- decided next, so the answer is a NAMED CONSTRAINT and not a missing row.
may_walk(P) :- person(P, adult).
may_walk(P) :- walks_alone(C, P), not waived(C).

attempt(T, drive(Dr, Cr), Dr, D, Dep, Ret) :-
    run(T, _, From, To, D, _, At), tt(From, To, M), driver(Dr), person(Dr, adult),
    car(Cr), not off(Dr, D), Dep is At - M, Ret is At + M.
attempt(T, walk(Ch), nobody, D, Dep, At) :-
    run(T, Ch, From, To, D, _, At), on_foot(From, To, M), may_walk(Ch), Dep is At - M.
offers(Cr, R, From, To, D, L, Arr, C) :-
    lift(C, Cr, R, From, To, Spec, L, Arr), applies_on(Spec, D),
    person(Cr, _), not off(Cr, D), not waived(C).
-- direction matters: a lift TO somewhere must arrive before the child is due,
-- a lift HOME must not leave before the child is free
attempt(T, lift(Cr), nobody, D, L, Arr) :-
    run(T, Ch, From, To, D, arrive_by, At), offers(Cr, Ch, From, To, D, L, Arr, _),
    Arr <= At.
attempt(T, lift(Cr), nobody, D, L, Arr) :-
    run(T, Ch, From, To, D, depart_at, At), offers(Cr, Ch, From, To, D, L, Arr, _),
    L >= At.

-- A LIFT YOU HAVE TO BE DRIVEN TO. The school bus leaves from the stop by the
-- neighbours and the child is at home; the model knew a lift (one hop) and a
-- drive (one hop) and had no way to SAY THE TWO TOGETHER, so the bus simply
-- never appeared among the ways and the morning looked like it had one
-- option. It has two, and which of them is open on a given day is the whole
-- question this model exists to answer.
--
-- Written as its own `attempt` rather than as a general path search: the
-- household knows its stops, and a two-hop composition is where a scheduler
-- turns into a router. If a third hop is ever needed, that is the moment to
-- stop and think, not to add another rule.
attempt(T, ferry(Dr, Stop, Giver), Dr, D, Dep, Ret) :-
    run(T, Ch, From, To, D, arrive_by, By),
    offers(Giver, Ch, Stop, To, D, Lv, Arr, _), Arr <= By,
    From != Stop, tt(From, Stop, M),
    driver(Dr), person(Dr, adult), car(_), not off(Dr, D),
    Dep is Lv - M, Ret is Lv.
-- A DROP-OFF ENDS WHEN THE RIDER IS DROPPED. `Ret is Lv + M` said the driver
-- goes home afterwards, and with that the model called this way closed for
-- the parent who actually does it every morning: he does not go home, he
-- carries straight on to the садик. Where the driver goes next is the rest of
-- the day's business, and the day already knows it - `at/5` has positions now.

-- ...AND THE SAME LIFT RUN BACKWARDS. The bus that leaves from the stop by
-- the neighbours RETURNS TO THAT STOP, and home is not walkable from it - so
-- the afternoon is `lift` then `drive`, exactly as the morning is `drive` then
-- `lift`. Written because the mirror was MISSING and its absence was silent:
-- the return lift had been recorded as ending at home, the composition was
-- never needed, and the model reported a school run that meets itself.
--
-- The two halves differ in where the driver's obligation ENDS. A drop-off ends
-- at the drop - `ferry` sets `Ret is Lv` because the parent carries on to the
-- садик. A pickup ends AT HOME: whoever meets the bus owes the ride back, so
-- `Ret is Arr + M` and the return minutes are part of what the way costs.
attempt(T, meet(Dr, Stop, Giver), Dr, D, Dep, Ret) :-
    run(T, Ch, From, To, D, depart_at, At),
    offers(Giver, Ch, From, Stop, D, Lv, Arr, _), Lv >= At,
    To != Stop, tt(Stop, To, M),
    driver(Dr), person(Dr, adult), car(_), not off(Dr, D),
    Dep is Arr - M, Ret is Arr + M.

-- ...AND A DROP-OFF YOU CAN BE LATE FOR. `can_wait(Stop)` says the stop is
-- somewhere a child may sit - the neighbours' porch, the school lobby - so the
-- bus landing does not APPOINT the pickup, it opens a window. That changes who
-- can do it: an adult busy at the moment the bus arrives and free an hour later
-- was being refused on the wrong grounds.
--
-- AND THE WINDOW HAS NO NUMBER IN IT. Asked how late the wait may run, the
-- household said "technically as late as you like, even till night, but of
-- course it would be unseemly" - so a hard limit would be an invention, and
-- picking one would smuggle a judgement into a fact file. What the model
-- computes instead is THE EARLIEST MOMENT EACH ADULT IS ACTUALLY FREE after
-- the drop, and how many minutes of somebody else's hospitality that costs.
-- The household reads the minutes and decides; nobody has to name a threshold
-- for "unseemly", which is exactly the kind of number that is wrong for every
-- family that is not the one who wrote it.
--
-- The extremum is taken by negation, the third time in this file: the earliest
-- free moment is the free moment nothing is earlier than. Candidates are the
-- drop itself and the END of every block that adult has that day, which is the
-- only place a busy adult can become free.
waitable_drop(T, D, Stop, Arr) :-
    run(T, Ch, From, To, D, depart_at, At),
    offers(Giver, Ch, From, Stop, D, Lv, Arr, _), Lv >= At, To != Stop,
    can_wait(Stop).

pick_at(T, Dr, D, Arr)   :- waitable_drop(T, D, _, Arr),
                            driver(Dr), person(Dr, adult), not off(Dr, D).
pick_at(T, Dr, D, X)     :- waitable_drop(T, D, _, Arr),
                            driver(Dr), person(Dr, adult), not off(Dr, D),
                            span(_, _, Dr, _, D, _, X), X > Arr.
-- BUSY FOR THE WHOLE ERRAND, not busy at the instant. Written the weak way
-- first and it was wrong in the expensive direction: an adult whose block ENDS
-- exactly at the drop counted as free at that minute, so the earliest pickup
-- came out equal to the drop for everybody and the late option was never
-- generated at all. The overlap test is the same one `way_blocked` uses.
trip_len(T, MM) :- waitable_drop(T, D, Stop, _),
                   run(T, _, _, To, D, depart_at, _), round_trip(Stop, To, MM).
pick_end(T, Dr, D, X, End) :- pick_at(T, Dr, D, X), trip_len(T, MM), End is X + MM.
pick_busy(T, Dr, D, X)   :- pick_end(T, Dr, D, X, End), span(_, _, Dr, _, D, F, Y),
                            X < Y, F < End.
pick_free(T, Dr, D, X)   :- pick_at(T, Dr, D, X), not pick_busy(T, Dr, D, X).
pick_later(T, Dr, D, X)  :- pick_free(T, Dr, D, X), pick_free(T, Dr, D, X2), X2 < X.
pick_first(T, Dr, D, X)  :- pick_free(T, Dr, D, X), not pick_later(T, Dr, D, X).

round_trip(A, B, MM) :- tt(A, B, M), MM is M + M.

attempt(T, wait_meet(Dr, Stop, Giver), Dr, D, Dep, Ret) :-
    waitable_drop(T, D, Stop, Arr),
    offers(Giver, _, _, Stop, D, _, Arr, _),
    run(T, _, _, To, D, depart_at, _), round_trip(Stop, To, MM),
    pick_first(T, Dr, D, Dep), car(_),
    Ret is Dep + MM.

-- WHOSE HOSPITALITY, AND HOW MUCH OF IT. The wait is a favour asked of a
-- person, not a property of a place, so it is reported with the host's name
-- and in minutes - the only two things anybody needs to judge "unseemly".
lands_at(T, Dr, Stop, D, L) :-
    attempt(T, wait_meet(Dr, Stop, _), Dr, D, Dep, _),
    run(T, _, _, To, D, depart_at, _), tt(Stop, To, M), L is Dep + M.
waited(T, Dr, Stop, D, Mins) :-
    lands_at(T, Dr, Stop, D, L), waitable_drop(T, D, Stop, Arr), Mins is L - Arr.
imposes_on(T, Dr, Host, D, Mins) :-
    waited(T, Dr, Stop, D, Mins), hosted(Stop, Host).

-- Nobody leaves before the household is awake, and a ferry that would need
-- them to is not a way. Written as a `way_blocked` so `whynot` names it.
way_blocked(T, ferry(Dr, Stop, Giver), before_waking) :-
    attempt(T, ferry(Dr, Stop, Giver), Dr, _, Dep, _), day_wake(Wake, _), Dep < Wake.

way_blocked(T, W, C) :- attempt(T, W, H, D, Dep, Ret), person(H, adult),
                        span(C, _, H, _, D, F, X), Dep < X, F < Ret.
blocked(T, W) :- way_blocked(T, W, _).
way(T, W)     :- attempt(T, W, _, _, _, _), not blocked(T, W).

-- =========================================================================
-- WHAT A WAY TAKES, AND WHICH WAYS CANNOT BOTH HAPPEN
-- =========================================================================
-- Three rules in this file were asking one question in three shapes:
-- `overbooked` (a person in two blocks), `teleport` (the car not where the
-- driver set off), and the plan enumeration in the host (two journeys, one
-- car). The question is A RESOURCE TAKEN BY TWO THINGS AT ONCE, and the only
-- thing that differs between them is WHICH resources a thing takes.
--
-- That difference is small and belongs to the CLASS, which is what makes this
-- worth generalising: a lift by somebody else takes nothing of ours, a drive
-- takes the driver and the car, a walk takes only the walker.
uses(T, W, H)  :- attempt(T, W, H, _, _, _), person(H, adult).
uses(T, W, Cr) :- attempt(T, W, H, _, _, _), person(H, adult), car(Cr), not has_cars(2).

-- INCOMPATIBLE IS PAIRWISE, and that is the whole reason this belongs in the
-- rules rather than in a host loop. Measured on this household: 25 open ways
-- make 300 pairs, against 1024 whole-day plans for the same week - and the
-- pairs stay quadratic while the plans stay exponential. A plan is then an
-- independent set over this relation, which anyone can ask for; nothing has
-- to enumerate plans to know which two cannot go together.
incompatible(T1, W1, T2, W2) :-
    attempt(T1, W1, _, D, F1, X1), attempt(T2, W2, _, D, F2, X2),
    T1 != T2, F1 < X2, F2 < X1,
    uses(T1, W1, R), uses(T2, W2, R).

-- ...and a trip whose every open way is incompatible with every open way of
-- another trip has no plan containing both. That is a QUESTION about the pair,
-- not a defect: which of the two gives is a household decision.
forced_apart(T1, T2) :-
    way(T1, _), way(T2, _), T1 != T2,
    not compatible_pair(T1, T2).
compatible_pair(T1, T2) :-
    way(T1, W1), way(T2, W2), not incompatible(T1, W1, T2, W2).
-- A TRIP NOBODY HAS TO MAKE, and one that somebody does. A lift given by
-- somebody else costs the household nothing; every other way costs an adult
-- half an hour. The distinction was invisible while a bus that stops short was
-- recorded as reaching the door, and that is precisely the case where it
-- matters - five weekday afternoons that looked free are five car trips.
free_ride(T)      :- way(T, W), attempt(T, W, nobody, _, _, _).
needs_a_driver(T) :- run(T, _, _, _, _, _, _), way(T, _), not free_ride(T).

run_ok(T)     :- way(T, _).
run_stuck(T)  :- run(T, _, _, _, _, _, _), not run_ok(T).

-- Fragility: exactly one way to make this run happen.
run_alt(T)     :- way(T, W1), way(T, W2), W1 != W2.
run_fragile(T) :- run_ok(T), not run_alt(T).

-- =========================================================================
-- 7. FREE TIME — derived, not read off as gaps
-- =========================================================================
-- A gap is not free when you are the only person who could cover a child in
-- it (on call even while idle), or when it sits inside a chain with no slack
-- (any overrun eats it). `raw_gap` is the naive reading, kept so the two can
-- be compared: the difference between them is the answer.

-- Gated on `asking(free)`, which `spat free` asserts. Materialising free
-- time for two adults over a whole week is a third of the model's cost and
-- nothing else reads it, so it is not computed unless somebody asks.
waking(P, D, S)    :- deep(slots), person(P, adult), slot(D, S), day_wake(F, T),
                      S >= F, S < T.
raw_gap(P, D, S)   :- asking(free), waking(P, D, S), not busy(P, D, S), not off(P, D).
free_slot(P, D, S) :- raw_gap(P, D, S), not sole(P, D, S), not in_tight_gap(P, D, S).
on_call(P, D, S)   :- raw_gap(P, D, S), sole(P, D, S).

-- =========================================================================
-- 8. WORKING HOURS, NOMINAL AND EFFECTIVE
-- =========================================================================
-- The kernel has no aggregation (START.md section 8), so the SUM is host-side
-- in spat.ts. What the rules decide is WHICH minutes count, and that is the
-- part with content in it: an hour of work while you are the only person a
-- child can turn to is not an hour of work.

work_slot(P, D, S) :- deep(slots), span(_, Ev, P, _, D, F, T), kind(Ev, work),
                      slot(D, S), S >= F, S < T.
eff_work(P, D, S)  :- work_slot(P, D, S), not sole(P, D, S).
diluted(P, D, S)   :- work_slot(P, D, S), sole(P, D, S).
-- and the weaker reading, which is the one the original schedule annotates
-- ("с Нико рядом — реально часа 3-4"): work done while a child is in the
-- house needing somebody, whether or not the other adult is also around.
shared_work(P, D, S) :- work_slot(P, D, S), on_duty(P, D, S).

-- THE DAILY TOTAL, IN RULES. The paragraph above says the sum is host-side
-- because the kernel has no aggregation. That is true of AGGREGATION and
-- FALSE OF SUMMING A SET: examples/slop folds a spreadsheet range with two
-- rules over a CHAIN --
--
--     total(I, V) :- sum_last(I, C), addend(C, V).
--     total(I, V) :- sum_item(I, C, J), addend(C, X), total(J, Y), V is X + Y.
--
-- -- and the same fold works here. One difference, in our favour: SLOP's HOST
-- has to lay the chain out, because a spreadsheet range is given by the text
-- of a formula and nothing in the sheet orders it. Here the order is
-- DERIVABLE, because blocks carry times, so the chain is rules too.
--
-- Only with the total inside the model can "не менее 7.5 часов в день" be a
-- constraint the week is CHECKED against, instead of a number a person reads
-- off a report and compares in their head.

work_blk(P, D, E, F, T) :- span(_, E, P, _, D, F, T), kind(E, work).

-- IMMEDIATE SUCCESSOR: E2 starts at or after E1 ends, and no third block sits
-- between them. `work_gap` is the two-hop composition, and negating it is
-- what turns a partial order into a chain.
work_after(P, D, E1, E2) :- work_blk(P, D, E1, _, T1), work_blk(P, D, E2, F2, _), F2 >= T1.
work_gap(P, D, E1, E2)   :- work_after(P, D, E1, E3), work_after(P, D, E3, E2).
work_next(P, D, E1, E2)  :- work_after(P, D, E1, E2), not work_gap(P, D, E1, E2).

work_has_next(P, D, E) :- work_next(P, D, E, _).
work_has_prev(P, D, E) :- work_next(P, D, _, E).
work_first(P, D, E)    :- work_blk(P, D, E, _, _), not work_has_prev(P, D, E).

-- the fold itself, the same two rules SLOP uses
work_run(P, D, E, M) :- work_blk(P, D, E, F, T), not work_has_next(P, D, E), M is T - F.
work_run(P, D, E, M) :- work_next(P, D, E, E2), work_blk(P, D, E, F, T),
                        work_run(P, D, E2, Y), M is T - F + Y.

day_work(P, D, M) :- work_first(P, D, E), work_run(P, D, E, M).

-- The requirement is a FACT in the week file, so a household that has no such
-- rule pays nothing: with no `work_needed` there is no `short_day` and the
-- reason never appears. Same shape as `give/2`.
short_day(P, D, M, N) :- work_needed(P, N), day_work(P, D, M), M < N.

-- =========================================================================
-- 9. BACKUP: who else could do this
-- =========================================================================
-- A responsibility with one possible discharger is a single point of failure;
-- with none it is already a hole. The counting semiring folded over
-- `run_ok/1` and over `covered/3` gives the number directly, one derivation
-- per person who could take it. Same metric NOPE uses for privilege sprawl,
-- with the sign inverted: there many paths is the alarm, here it is the
-- reassurance.

-- =========================================================================
-- 10. WHERE COULD SOMETHING NEW GO
-- =========================================================================
-- The one query that GENERATES rather than evaluates. `want/4` is asserted by
-- `spat place`; with no want fact none of this costs anything. Candidates are
-- every grid start that fits in the day, filtered by exactly the rules that
-- decide everything else — so a placement is admissible on the same terms.

cand(A, D, S) :- want(A, _, _, Dur), slot(D, S), day_end(E), End is S + Dur, End <= E.
cand_slot(A, D, S, S2) :- cand(A, D, S), want(A, _, _, Dur), End is S + Dur,
                          slot(D, S2), S2 >= S, S2 < End.
cand_bad(A, D, S, owner_busy)  :- cand_slot(A, D, S, S2), want(A, P, _, _), busy(P, D, S2).
cand_bad(A, D, S, sole_cover)  :- cand_slot(A, D, S, S2), want(A, P, _, _), sole(P, D, S2).
cand_bad(A, D, S, owner_away)  :- cand(A, D, S), want(A, P, _, _), off(P, D).
cand_bad(A, D, S, asleep)      :- cand_slot(A, D, S, S2), want(A, P, _, _), not waking(P, D, S2).
admissible(A, D, S) :- cand(A, D, S), not cand_bad(A, D, S, _).

-- =========================================================================
-- 11. DOES THE WEEK HOLD TOGETHER
-- =========================================================================
-- Feasibility is the wider question and coverage is one component of it.

-- A PERSON IN TWO PLACES is caught by `overbooked` below and by
-- `negative_slack` above: one for overlap, one for not enough time to get
-- between them. THE CAR IS NOT, and that is a different problem, because a
-- person's position is DECLARED by their blocks while a car's has to be
-- worked out from who last drove it.
--
-- The cheap half of it is exact and needs no positions at all: two people
-- travelling AT THE SAME TIME need two vehicles. A leg is the gap between two
-- blocks at different places - the model already knows both ends.

-- WHERE SOMEBODY IS, AND FROM WHEN TO WHEN. The day is a sequence of
-- positions and the moves between them - which is what the household says
-- when it describes a morning, and what this model did not have. Three
-- separate patches stood here instead: legs only for drivers, legs that were
-- not the whole gap, legs for the first trip out of the house. All three were
-- symptoms of the same absence, and all three go away once position exists.
--
-- INTERVALS, NOT A GRID. The slot grid costs about a thousand facts and sits
-- behind `deep`; positions are a handful per person per day and are the same
-- language the rest of the model already speaks.

at(P, D, Pl, F, T) :- span(_, _, P, Pl, D, F, T).
-- and at home before the first commitment and after the last
-- HOME ENDS WHEN YOU LEAVE, not when you arrive somewhere else. The first
-- version ended it at the next block's start, so the model had a person at
-- home at 07:55 and at the садик at 07:55 - teleporting, and every leg came
-- out zero minutes long.
at(P, D, Home, Wake, Lv) :-
    base(Home), span(_, E, P, Pl, D, F, _), person(P, _), Pl != Home,
    not has_prev(P, D, E), day_wake(Wake, _), tt(Home, Pl, M),
    Lv is F - M, Wake < Lv.
at(P, D, Home, Back, Sleep) :-
    base(Home), span(_, E, P, Pl, D, _, T), person(P, _), Pl != Home,
    not has_next(P, D, E), day_wake(_, Sleep), tt(Pl, Home, M),
    Back is T + M, Back < Sleep.

-- A MOVE IS BETWEEN CONSECUTIVE POSITIONS, which is what makes a CHAIN work:
-- Сейидовы then straight on to the садик, without the invented trip home that
-- made the model call that morning impossible. Nothing here says "return".
-- RANGE-RESTRICTED: `A` and `B` are the two ends the leg rule compares, and
-- they must be BOUND by positions, not merely constrained by them. Written
-- the other way this rule is unsafe and the engine unfolds it top-down.
at_between(P, D, A, B) :-
    at(P, D, _, _, A), at(P, D, _, B, _), A < B,
    at(P, D, _, F, _), F > A, F < B.
leg(P, D, From, To, Dep, Arr, Need) :-
    driver(P), at(P, D, From, _, Dep), at(P, D, To, Arr, _),
    From != To, Dep <= Arr, not at_between(P, D, Dep, Arr), tt(From, To, Need).

-- A CAR IS A RESOURCE WITH A POSITION, exactly like the people are. Two
-- people and a car are three things that can each be in one place at a time,
-- and the model had position for the first two and nothing for the third.
-- What it had instead was a clash rule over TIME OVERLAP, and that is a
-- weaker and different question: it asked whether two journeys fit end to
-- end, never whether the car was where the driver started.
--
-- Found by looking at a printed table and asking "а машина к ней
-- телепортируется?" - one plan had robin drive to the stop at 07:30 and
-- alex set off from home at 07:40 in the same car. Time overlap said fine.
--
-- HOW FAR THIS REACHES: one car, exactly. With two, which car a journey takes
-- is an assignment, and an assignment is a search - which belongs in the host
-- with the rest of the enumeration, not here. Stated so an audit can read it.
car_check_covers(1).
has_cars(2) :- car(A), car(B), A != B.

-- Every journey by a driver takes the car; there is nothing else to take.
car_leg(Cr, D, From, To, Dep, Arr) :-
    car(Cr), driver(P), leg(P, D, From, To, Dep, Arr, _), not has_cars(2).

-- Where the car is, and from when: at home when the day starts, and wherever
-- the last journey left it.
car_here(Cr, D, Home, Wake) :- car(Cr), base(Home), day(D, _), day_wake(Wake, _).
car_here(Cr, D, To, Arr)    :- car_leg(Cr, D, _, To, _, Arr).

-- ...and it is still there only if nobody drove it away in between.
-- RANGE-RESTRICTED: both ends come from positions and journeys, not from
-- comparisons. Fourth time this session that an interval got written with its
-- bounds only constrained; the tell is a variable that appears nowhere but in
-- a `<` or `>=`.
car_taken(Cr, D, T0, T) :-
    car_here(Cr, D, _, T0), car_leg(Cr, D, _, _, T, _), T0 <= T,
    car_leg(Cr, D, _, _, Dep, _), Dep >= T0, Dep < T.
car_stands(Cr, D, Pl, T) :-
    car_here(Cr, D, Pl, T0), car_leg(Cr, D, _, _, T, _), T0 <= T,
    not car_taken(Cr, D, T0, T).

-- =========================================================================
-- EVERY JOURNEY HAS MODES, AND AN ADULT'S TOO
-- =========================================================================
-- A child's journey had alternatives (`run` + `way`); an adult's had none -
-- `leg` simply said "he drove". That asymmetry is why walking had to be
-- bolted on beside the model as a special mechanism, and why the household
-- could not ask "could you walk instead" about a grown-up.
--
-- So: a mode is available when its requirements are met, and the ONLY part
-- that differs between modes is what they need and what they take. Driving
-- needs the car to be where you set off from; walking needs a walking time
-- and takes nobody else's anything.
goes(P, D, From, To, Dep, Arr, drive) :-
    leg(P, D, From, To, Dep, Arr, _), driver(P), car(Cr), not has_cars(2),
    car_stands(Cr, D, From, Dep).
-- With a fleet, WHICH car a journey takes is an assignment and this check
-- stands down - `car_check_covers(1)` says so. Without this second rule the
-- mode vanished entirely at two cars and every journey read as a teleport:
-- the boundary was written into the OPTION instead of into the check.
goes(P, D, From, To, Dep, Arr, drive) :-
    leg(P, D, From, To, Dep, Arr, _), driver(P), has_cars(2).
goes(P, D, From, To, Dep, Arr, walk) :-
    leg(P, D, From, To, Dep, Arr, _), may_walk(P), foot(From, To, _).

-- WHAT A MODE TAKES. Same shape as `uses/3` for a way, and the same reason:
-- this is the whole class-specific part of the model.
takes(P, D, Dep, drive, P)  :- goes(P, D, _, _, Dep, _, drive).
takes(P, D, Dep, drive, Cr) :- goes(P, D, _, _, Dep, _, drive), car(Cr), not has_cars(2).
takes(P, D, Dep, walk,  P)  :- goes(P, D, _, _, Dep, _, walk).

-- THE TELEPORT IS NOW A VIEW: driving is simply not among the modes. What was
-- a bespoke check becomes the absence of an option, which is the difference
-- between a rule that says "this is wrong" and a model that says "this is not
-- available".
teleport(P, D, From, Dep) :-
    leg(P, D, From, To, Dep, Arr, _), driver(P),
    not goes(P, D, From, To, Dep, Arr, drive).


-- WORK YIELDS. "Если я куда-то еду - я в это время не работаю": a working
-- day is not a commitment that competes with the dentist, it is what is left
-- when the dentist and the drive there have taken their minutes. Counting a
-- работа/приём overlap as being in two places at once reported four
-- collisions on this household, and every one of them was the same false
-- shape: the appointment is real, the work block is elastic around it.
--
-- So an overlap is a defect only between two blocks that BOTH insist.
overbooked(P, D, E1, E2) :- span(_, E1, P, _, D, F1, T1), span(_, E2, P, _, D, F2, T2),
                            E1 != E2, F1 < T2, F2 < T1,
                            not kind(E1, work), not kind(E2, work).
broken(double_booked) :- overbooked(_, _, _, _).
broken(uncovered)     :- uncovered(_, _, _).
broken(no_time)       :- negative_slack(_, _, _, _).
broken(no_way)        :- run_stuck(_).
broken(short_day)     :- short_day(_, _, _, _).
broken(missed_window) :- too_early(_, _, _, _, _).
broken(missed_window) :- too_late(_, _, _, _, _).
-- A CAR COLLISION IS NOT A DEFECT, it is a MODE SWITCH. "У нас нет
-- опциональности: если машина не заблокирована - едет, если заблокирована -
-- идёт пешком." So the model must not offer a choice it does not have; it
-- must follow the rule and then ask whether the slower journey still fits.
-- A TELEPORT IS NOT A DEFECT, IT IS THE MODE SWITCH. The car not being there
-- means you walk; whether that still works is `too_slow`'s question. Making
-- the teleport itself a failure would be the same mistake as making the old
-- car collision one - reporting the branch instead of its consequence.
-- =========================================================================
-- PASSENGERS: a child moves because somebody takes them
-- =========================================================================
-- The table had the child as `·` for the fifteen minutes of the journey -
-- present before, present after, nowhere in between - because `leg` is for
-- drivers and a child is not one. That is the same hole the car had an hour
-- ago, in the third of three places: PEOPLE, THE CAR, AND THE CHILDREN are
-- one kind of thing, and each of them can be in one place at a time.
--
-- A child rode along if an adult made exactly that journey while the child's
-- own position changed across it. Nothing is asserted about seats or who sat
-- where - the model says WHO WAS TAKEN BY WHOM, which is what a household
-- argues about.
passenger(Ch, P, D, From, To, Dep, Arr) :-
    leg(P, D, From, To, Dep, Arr, _), person(Ch, child),
    at(Ch, D, From, _, Dep), at(Ch, D, To, Arr, _).

-- ...or somebody else's vehicle took them, which the model already knew about
carried_by(Ch, Giver, D, From, To, Lv, Arr) :-
    offers(Giver, Ch, From, To, D, Lv, Arr, _),
    at(Ch, D, From, _, Lv), at(Ch, D, To, Arr, _).

-- A CHILD WHOSE POSITION CHANGES WITH NOBODY TAKING THEM. Kept as a QUESTION
-- rather than a defect, and the reason is worth writing down: it measures the
-- gap between TWO ACCOUNTS OF THE SAME JOURNEY that this model now carries.
--
--   `run` + `way`      the trip must happen, and here is how it could go -
--                      which knows the bus leaves from the stop
--   `at` + `passenger` he was here, then there, and X took him -
--                      which only sees the endpoints
--
-- Measured: 15 movements look unaccompanied under the second account while
-- `run_stuck` - the first account's own verdict - is ZERO. The child is not
-- stranded; the two accounts simply do not talk. Which way was taken is a
-- CHOICE, and choices live in the host with the rest of the enumeration, so
-- `passenger` can only name the taker once a plan has fixed one.
--
-- So this is not `broken`. Making it broken would report the model's own
-- seam as a defect in somebody's week.
moved_alone(Ch, D, From, To, Dep) :-
    person(Ch, child), at(Ch, D, From, _, Dep), at(Ch, D, To, Arr, _),
    From != To, Dep < Arr,
    not passenger(Ch, _, D, From, To, Dep, Arr),
    not carried_by(Ch, _, D, From, To, Dep, Arr),
    not walks_alone(Ch, D).
walks_alone(Ch, D) :- walks_alone(C, Ch), not waived(C), day(D, _).

-- =========================================================================
-- WHEN THE CAR IS NOT THERE: THE WALK
-- =========================================================================
-- "У нас нет опциональности. Если машина не заблокирована - Робин едет, если
-- заблокирована - идёт пешком. Пешком на дорогу уходит больше времени."
--
-- A journey that starts where the car is NOT is exactly the blocked case, so
-- the fallback hangs off `teleport`. The two halves were built a day apart
-- and turn out to be one rule: giving the car a position produced the walking
-- rule for free, and the time-overlap heuristic it replaced could not have.
on_foot_leg(P, D, From, To, Dep, Arr) :-
    teleport(P, D, From, Dep), leg(P, D, From, To, Dep, Arr, _).

-- Walking is symmetric the way driving is: the household writes the pair once.
foot(A, B, M) :- on_foot(A, B, M).
foot(B, A, M) :- on_foot(A, B, M), A != B.

-- SOME LEGS CANNOT BE WALKED AT ALL, and that is a different fact from not
-- knowing how long. "Возле них ближайшая остановка, но пешком оттуда не дойти,
-- надо ехать на машине" - no number makes that journey possible, so the
-- fallback must not go looking for one.
cannot_walk(A, B) :- no_walk(A, B).
cannot_walk(B, A) :- no_walk(A, B), A != B.

walk_impossible(P, D, F, T) :- on_foot_leg(P, D, F, T, _, _), cannot_walk(F, T).
walk_unknown(P, D, F, T)    :- on_foot_leg(P, D, F, T, _, _),
                               not foot(F, T, _), not cannot_walk(F, T).

-- HOW MUCH ROOM THERE REALLY IS: from the end of whatever came before - and
-- work does not count, because work yields - to the appointment. Comparing
-- against the DRIVING window would be circular: that window is the car's.
room_before(P, D, Arr, End) :-
    on_foot_leg(P, D, _, _, _, Arr),
    span(_, E, P, _, D, _, End), End <= Arr, not kind(E, work),
    not room_later(P, D, Arr, End).
room_later(P, D, Arr, End) :-
    on_foot_leg(P, D, _, _, _, Arr),
    span(_, E, P, _, D, _, End), End <= Arr, not kind(E, work),
    span(_, E2, P, _, D, _, End2), End2 <= Arr, End2 > End, not kind(E2, work).
room_before(P, D, Arr, Wake) :-
    on_foot_leg(P, D, _, _, _, Arr), person(P, adult), day_wake(Wake, _),
    not room_any(P, D, Arr).
room_any(P, D, Arr) :-
    on_foot_leg(P, D, _, _, _, Arr),
    span(_, E, P, _, D, _, End), End <= Arr, not kind(E, work).

-- ONLY A JOURNEY TO SOMETHING CAN BE LATE. The way home has no appointment at
-- the end of it: walking back simply gets you home later.
too_slow(P, D, F, T) :- on_foot_leg(P, D, F, T, _, Arr), base(Home), T != Home,
                        foot(F, T, M),
                        room_before(P, D, Arr, Start), Room is Arr - Start, Room < M.

broken(cannot_get_there) :- too_slow(_, _, _, _).
broken(on_foot_unknown)  :- walk_unknown(_, _, _, _).
broken(no_way_on_foot)   :- walk_impossible(_, _, _, _).
broken(travel_ambiguous) :- two_ways(_, _, _, _).
holds_together(week)  :- not broken(_).

-- =========================================================================
-- 12. THE HOUSEHOLD'S OWN RULES — the extension points
-- =========================================================================
-- A household writes rules of its own into its volume (`spat rule add`,
-- examples/spat/rules.ts): a new KIND of constraint — «нужен ноутбук»,
-- «согласовать время» — without a release. They live in a book of their own,
-- [hh], and the loader stamps every head of that book [hh] whatever the text
-- said, so nothing written there can conclude into main. EXACTLY FOUR
-- relations of the book are read — these two here, `defect[hh]` in
-- access.rofl, and `warn[hh](Kind, Text, Day)` by the host as a «!!» line of
-- tomorrow/show; everything else the book concludes is inert. The book reads
-- the world; main reads the book — both declared, so the flow audit is quiet.
imports(main, hh).
imports(hh, main).
-- for: a family rule says someone is committed — gated as `busy` is
busy(P, D, S)         :- deep(slots), busy[hh](P, D, S).
-- for: a family rule says a child needs somebody at a slot — the same
--      `uncovered` then asks who is on duty
needs_cover(Ch, D, S) :- needs_cover[hh](Ch, D, S).

-- =========================================================================
-- 13. DECLARED INPUTS — do not edit, do not delete
-- =========================================================================
-- Every relation a week file supplies, declared extensional. Without this a
-- relation read under `not` with neither facts nor rules gets no stratum
-- (LIMITS.md), and boot's `undefined_premise` audit correctly reads an
-- unpopulated positive premise as a misspelling. So a half-written week file
-- — no lifts yet, nobody away, no overrides — loads clean and answers,
-- instead of failing in a way nobody can see.

edb(day).             edb(in_group).       edb(place).        edb(base).
edb(supervised).      edb(travel).         edb(on_foot).
edb(person).          edb(driver).         edb(car).          edb(walks_alone).
edb(constraint).      edb(give).           edb(kind).         edb(with).
edb(week).            edb(current).
edb(usual).           edb(usual_on).       edb(moved).        edb(skipped).
edb(added).
edb(present_window).  edb(absent).         edb(absent_on).    edb(awake).
edb(present_on).
edb(lift).            edb(day_wake).
edb(want).            edb(waived).         edb(asking).
edb(work_needed).
edb(travel_as).       edb(assume).
edb(ru_name).
edb(must_start).
edb(never_alone).
edb(no_walk).        edb(ride).       edb(can_wait).   edb(hosted).

-- =========================================================================
-- 14. THE FAMILY'S BOOK EXTENDS THE WORLD — the declarations
-- =========================================================================
-- «Весь пойнт ROFL — грамматика расширяется на ходу под задачу, и
-- нижележащие книги не ломают ранее определённого.» A fact of the family's
-- book [hh] (`spat rule add 'place(academy). travel(home, academy, 15).'`)
-- whose relation is declared `edb` above is one more line of the world:
-- MONOTONE — added, never overriding. `examples/spat/bridge.rofl` is GENERATED
-- from this section by examples/spat/bridge.ts (the demo refuses a stale
-- file): per relation, `R(...) :- R[hh](...), not world_R(<key>)`, where
-- `world_R` is read off the kernel's own trail (`asserted_by`) of what the
-- world book asserted, and `overrides[audit](R, k(<key>))` names a family
-- fact whose key the world already has with another value — ignored, and
-- `rule add` says so. Every edb relation is decided here, one line each; an
-- edb relation with neither `key_of` nor `not_extended` stops the generator.
--
-- key_of(R, N): the first N arguments identify a fact; N = 0 is a singleton
-- (one base, one current week), N = arity is a set (a second row is never a
-- conflict).
key_of(day, 1).            key_of(in_group, 2).       key_of(place, 1).        key_of(base, 0).
key_of(supervised, 1).     key_of(travel, 2).         key_of(on_foot, 2).
key_of(person, 1).         key_of(driver, 1).         key_of(car, 1).          key_of(walks_alone, 1).
key_of(constraint, 1).     key_of(give, 1).           key_of(kind, 1).         key_of(with, 2).
key_of(current, 0).
key_of(present_window, 1). key_of(absent, 1).         key_of(awake, 1).
key_of(lift, 1).           key_of(day_wake, 0).
key_of(work_needed, 1).
key_of(travel_as, 3).      key_of(assume, 0).
key_of(ru_name, 1).
key_of(must_start, 1).
key_of(never_alone, 1).
key_of(no_walk, 2).        key_of(ride, 4).           key_of(can_wait, 1).     key_of(hosted, 1).
-- not_extended(R, Why): what the book does NOT put into the world. `books`:
-- the week's layers are the members' books through access.rofl's rights
-- (add/skip/move, every); a family fact here would skip the school without
-- the right to. `query`: a switch a verb asserts for one call (relax, free,
-- place), not a fact of the household.
not_extended(usual, books).    not_extended(usual_on, books).  not_extended(moved, books).
not_extended(skipped, books).  not_extended(added, books).     not_extended(absent_on, books).
not_extended(present_on, books).
not_extended(want, query).     not_extended(asking, query).    not_extended(waived, query).
-- `weeks`: a week is the operator's — it comes with its Monday (`week_starts`, a
-- book relation of access.rofl) and a `roll`; a week the book named alone
-- would be a week with no date under it
not_extended(week, weeks).
-- extends_unless(R, I, V): argument I may not be V. An adult or a helper is
-- an ACCOUNT — who may read which book is decided over world + users before
-- any book is opened (STORE.md, 2), and a guest made `adult` by one line of
-- a family rule would read every book. Children, neighbours, services and
-- visitors are the schedule's people and extend; accounts are the operator's.
extends_unless(person, 2, adult).  extends_unless(person, 2, helper).
-- speaks_for(R, I): argument I is a person the fact speaks for. The book is
-- an adult's (rule add), and an adult speaks for adults and children, never
-- for a helper (§3 of access.rofl): a helper's hours are hers to report.
speaks_for(driver, 1).         speaks_for(walks_alone, 2).   speaks_for(with, 2).
speaks_for(present_window, 2). speaks_for(absent, 2).        speaks_for(awake, 2).
speaks_for(lift, 3).           speaks_for(work_needed, 1).   speaks_for(never_alone, 1).
speaks_for(hosted, 2).
-- key_owned(R, G): a key G already owns — an edit's id has its constraint
-- from its book (access.rofl §4), and a family fact may not give it another
key_owned(constraint, edit_by).
